40,2,1,talk,Shion,,"The data confirms my suspicions. The maintenance logs are skewed, and the office is becoming a dumping ground for crumbs and waste because people are eating at their desks. The lack of a proper break room is bleeding into our workspace.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I found out that we've spent 68,000 yen on outside room hires last year. It seems we're paying a lot of money because we don't have a space that works for us right now.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Rin,,"Wow, I found some really interesting stuff! Only two people are using the room almost all the time, and everyone else hardly ever goes in! Plus, someone said something super awkward right in front of everyone! That's so scary!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Servas,,"It is revealing that our interactions have dwindled to the printer's side. We are physically close, yet we have become strangers. A room is more than its walls; it is a vessel for the bonds we forge.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The data is telling. The room's usage is heavily skewed toward those nearest the door, while the facilities logs show a significant amount of maintenance effort is being spent on equipment rooms. We are wasting both time and space.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Toshio, the ""waste"" isn't just in the logs. The fact that people are eating at their desks—leaving crumbs in the keyboards—proves the current room is failing as a break space. It's a hygiene issue that affects us all.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@Shion, I... I understand. And looking at the quotes, a proper kitchen setup would be 96,000 yen, while a meeting setup is 141,000. It's a lot of money, but maybe it's better than paying for outside rooms every month.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Rin,,"@Daisuke, that's a lot of money! But if we make the room super inviting, maybe everyone will use it instead of just the two people by the door! I don't want anyone to be scared of awkward talks anymore!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Rin, your heart is in the right place. However, we must also consider the need for a sanctuary. I have discovered that our current walls are far too thin to protect the privacy of a difficult conversation.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Servas, privacy is a luxury, but inefficiency is a cost. If the room is only being used by two people, we are maintaining a space that serves almost no one. We must prioritize a function that maximizes utility without increasing the labor burden.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Servas, you've hit on the core issue. Thin walls and a lack of boundaries mean the noise and mess from that room spill directly into our workspace. It's not about ""sanctuary""; it's about basic professional stability.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"@Toshio, I... I agree that utility is important. But if we keep paying for outside rooms because we can't meet here, isn't that a waste too? I'm just worried about who has to sign for the costs.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Rin,,"@Toshio, but if only two people use it, maybe it's because the others are too shy or it's not fun! If we make a rule about how to use it and clean it, everyone can join the party! Don't you think?","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Servas,,"@Rin, rules can provide order, but they rarely inspire the heart. We must create a space where people feel naturally drawn to one another, allowing us to speak the truths that currently remain unspoken.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Rin, rules are exactly what we need to prevent the ""party"" from becoming someone else's chore. If we don't define the labor, the upkeep will inevitably fall on a few, wasting their time and energy.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Shion,,"@Toshio, you're focusing on the labor of the room, but you're ignoring the labor of the office. Cleaning crumbs out of keyboards is a chore we're already doing because the break room is a failure. We need a solution that stops the spillover.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think Shion is right. If we don't fix the room, the mess just moves elsewhere. But I'm still worried about the budget. We're already paying for outside rooms, and these fit-out quotes are quite high.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Rin,,"@Daisuke, don't be so worried! If we stop paying for those outside rooms, we can use that money for the fit-out! It's like a magic trade! Let's just make sure we have a fair way to keep it clean!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Servas,,"@Rin, your spirit of generosity is a light for us all. Let us remember that the most valuable investment we can make is in each other. A space that fosters genuine connection is worth every yen.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"@Servas, sentiment does not pay for maintenance. @Daisuke, the ""magic trade"" Rin suggests only works if the new setup doesn't create a permanent labor drain. We must choose the option that minimizes both financial and human costs.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Servas"",""Daisuke"",""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,,status,Daisuke,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
61,3,,status,Toshio,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
